-- Update the published English Insight after migration 039.
-- Keep migration 039 immutable; this migration carries the editorial revision into PostgreSQL.

BEGIN;

UPDATE cms_insights
SET
  title = "MVP vs Prototype vs Proof of Concept: Which Should Your Business Choose?",
  excerpt = "MVP, Prototype, and Proof of Concept are not three versions of the same product. They are different validation tools for different uncertainties. This practical guide explains what each one proves, what it cannot prove, what evidence to collect, and how to choose the right approach before you commit more budget and engineering effort.",
  content = $$[{"type":"paragraph","text":"When a company wants to build a new digital product, the conversation often starts with features, technology, timelines, and budget. Those things matter, but they are not the best first question."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The better starting point is: <strong>what could make this product fail, and what do we still not know?</strong>"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"If the biggest unknown is whether users can understand a workflow, a Prototype may be enough. If the workflow is clear but you do not know whether people will actually use and value it, an MVP is more appropriate. If a critical integration, data source, architecture, or performance requirement has never been proven, a Proof of Concept may be the most valuable first step."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"That distinction matters because Prototype, MVP, and Proof of Concept are not simply three levels of the same product. They are different experiments designed to reduce different kinds of uncertainty."},{"type":"callout","tone":"info","title":"The rule that matters most","text":"Choose the smallest thing that can answer your biggest uncertainty. Do not build more simply because a larger deliverable feels safer."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"MVP vs Prototype vs Proof of Concept: the 30-second answer"},{"type":"table","headers":["If you need to know...","Start with...","What you are trying to prove"],"rows":[["Can users understand the flow and complete the task?","Prototype","Usability, interaction, information hierarchy, and experience"],["Will users actually use and value the core product?","MVP","Behavioral evidence and early product value"],["Can the technical approach work under the real constraints?","Proof of Concept","Technical feasibility"],["Could several risks invalidate the project?","A combination","Separate evidence for each critical uncertainty"]]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This is a decision framework, not a mandatory sequence. A team may run a technical PoC before any prototype, a prototype before an MVP, two experiments in parallel, or skip a method entirely when the relevant uncertainty has already been reduced."},{"type":"image","url":"/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/validate.svg","alt":"Prototype validates user experience, MVP validates product value, and Proof of Concept validates technical feasibility","caption":"The methods differ because the questions differ: experience, product value, and technical feasibility."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Why choosing the wrong validation method gets expensive"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The costly mistake is often not choosing the wrong framework or programming language. It is investing in the wrong level of validation before the most important question has been answered."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A team can spend weeks engineering an MVP when the real issue is a confusing workflow. It can spend days polishing a high-fidelity prototype when the real risk is an API that may not support the required data or latency. It can also build a PoC that proves a technology works in isolation while never testing whether the solution creates useful business value."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Good validation reduces uncertainty in an intentional order. The scope should follow the decision you need to make, not the other way around."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"What is a Prototype?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A prototype is a representation of a proposed solution used to explore, communicate, or test how that solution behaves from the user's perspective. It can be a rough wireframe, a clickable flow, or a high-fidelity interface that closely resembles the intended product."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Prototype testing is especially useful when the main uncertainty sits in <strong>how people will interact with the solution</strong>. You can test navigation, information hierarchy, task flow, labels, interaction states, and whether the concept makes sense before committing to production engineering."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Nielsen Norman Group defines prototype testing as usability testing where the interface being tested is a design prototype rather than a live product. It can be used to identify usability problems before implementation or to compare alternative design solutions. [Read the reference](https://media.nngroup.com/media/articles/attachments/glossary-ux-research.pdf)."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"What a Prototype can tell you"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["Whether users understand the intended workflow.","Where people hesitate, misinterpret labels, or take the wrong path.","Whether the information hierarchy supports the task.","Whether important states, errors, and decisions are visible at the right time.","Which of several interface approaches is easier to understand.","Whether stakeholders share the same mental model of the product."]},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"What a Prototype cannot prove"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["That the production backend will scale.","That a third-party integration will work under production constraints.","That users will pay for the product.","That real-world adoption or retention will be strong.","That the final infrastructure, security model, and operational setup are ready."]},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"What should you measure in a Prototype test?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The metrics depend on the question. For a critical workflow, useful evidence can include task success, time on task, navigation errors, points of confusion, first-click behavior, and qualitative feedback. Nielsen Norman Group also identifies measures such as task success, satisfaction, and task time as common quantitative usability measures. [See the research glossary](https://media.nngroup.com/media/articles/attachments/glossary-ux-research.pdf)."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Do not collect metrics just because they are available. Pick evidence that helps you decide whether the experience should be changed before engineering moves further."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"For a booking platform, for example, a Prototype can test search, date selection, availability discovery, checkout steps, and error recovery without connecting a real payment gateway or live inventory system."},{"type":"image","url":"/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/questions.svg","alt":"Questions that separate Prototype, MVP, and Proof of Concept","caption":"Start from the question. The question determines how much product, code, or infrastructure you actually need."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"What is an MVP?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"An MVP, or Minimum Viable Product, is a product version designed to generate <strong>validated learning</strong> from real use with the smallest meaningful scope. Eric Ries defines an MVP as a version that lets a team collect the maximum amount of validated learning about customers with the least effort. [Read the original definition](https://leanstartup.co/resources/articles/what-is-an-mvp/)."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The word minimum is often misunderstood. An MVP is not a broken product, a random collection of features, or a final product with half of its screens removed. The scope is minimum because you are deliberately building only what is required to test a specific product hypothesis."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The word viable matters just as much. The product needs enough quality, reliability, and completeness in its core flow to generate a signal you can trust. If the experience is intentionally unusable, you may be measuring the consequences of poor execution rather than the hypothesis itself."},{"type":"image","url":"/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/learning-loop.svg","alt":"MVP learning loop from problem and core solution to real users, feedback, learning, and iteration","caption":"An MVP is a learning loop. The product is the instrument used to collect evidence and decide what to build next."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"What makes an MVP different from a Prototype?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A Prototype primarily tests an experience. An MVP tests a product hypothesis through real product behavior. The difference is not whether something is built in Figma or code. The difference is the kind of evidence you need."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A clickable prototype may tell you that users can understand an approval workflow. An MVP can tell you whether teams actually use that workflow, how often they return, where they drop out, whether the workflow saves time, or whether another measurable behavior changes."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"What should you measure in an MVP?"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["Activation: do target users reach the core value moment?","Core-task completion: can users complete the job the product is designed to improve?","Repeat usage or retention: do users come back when the problem recurs?","Conversion or willingness to pay: when monetization is part of the hypothesis, do users take the relevant commercial action?","Operational impact: does the product reduce time, errors, manual work, or another meaningful business cost?","Qualitative learning: why are users adopting, ignoring, abandoning, or working around the product?"]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"There is no universal MVP metric or success threshold. The evidence should be tied to the hypothesis and business context. An internal operations product may care more about cycle time and error reduction than page views. A consumer product may care more about activation and repeat usage. A B2B product may need evidence that the workflow creates enough operational value for a team to keep using it."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"What an MVP does not automatically prove"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"An MVP can provide evidence of product value without proving that the business model is fully validated, that the product can scale to enterprise volume, or that the production system is hardened for every operational scenario. Those are separate questions."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"What is a Proof of Concept?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A Proof of Concept (PoC) is a focused experiment designed to determine whether a technical approach is feasible under relevant constraints. The question may involve an API, data source, architecture, integration, performance requirement, AI capability, infrastructure pattern, or another technical dependency."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The Australian Government's guidance on AI proof of concepts describes a PoC as a focused, small-scale experiment intended to demonstrate technical feasibility and potential business value before full-scale integration. It also emphasizes a clear problem statement, success criteria, testing, and a pathway beyond the experiment. [Read the guidance](https://www.digital.gov.au/policy/ai/AI-POC-to-scale/context-and-principles)."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"A good PoC has five boundaries"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":["A precise technical question: what are you trying to prove?","A defined boundary: what is deliberately out of scope?","Representative conditions: what data, integration, workload, or environment makes the test credible?","Success criteria: what result would count as technically acceptable?","A decision: what will you do if the answer is yes, no, or inconclusive?"]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A useful PoC is often intentionally disposable. Its purpose is to remove a high-risk unknown before the team commits to a larger architecture or production implementation. The point is evidence, not code volume."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"What can a PoC measure?"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["Latency, throughput, concurrency, or response characteristics.","Error rates, reliability, and failure modes.","Data quality, completeness, or compatibility.","Model accuracy, relevance, or other task-specific technical metrics.","Integration compatibility with existing systems.","Resource usage or estimated operating cost.","Security, authentication, privacy, or infrastructure constraints that could block the intended approach."]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"IBM also recommends evaluating new technologies against practical criteria such as capability, compatibility with existing systems, alignment with business objectives, and cost, with proof-of-concept work used to demonstrate feasibility where appropriate. [See IBM's guidance](https://www.ibm.com/think/insights/guide-to-experimenting-new-ai-technologies)."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"What a PoC does not prove"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A successful PoC does not automatically mean the solution is production-ready. It may still require architecture work, security hardening, monitoring, governance, UX design, operational processes, or a much broader test with real users."},{"type":"image","url":"/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/comparison.svg","alt":"Comparison of Prototype, MVP, and Proof of Concept across users, evidence, UX fidelity, technical depth, and readiness","caption":"Prototype, MVP, and PoC optimize for different evidence. None is a universal shortcut to production."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"MVP vs Prototype vs PoC: a practical comparison"},{"type":"table","headers":["Dimension","Prototype","MVP","PoC"],"rows":[["Primary question","Can people use and understand it?","Will people use and value it?","Can we technically make it work?"],["Primary uncertainty","Desirability / usability","Product value / early viability","Technical feasibility"],["Typical evidence","Task behavior, usability findings, design feedback","Usage, retention, conversion, operational impact, qualitative learning","Performance, integration, data, accuracy, reliability"],["Real users","Useful and often important","Usually essential","Usually limited or not the primary focus"],["Engineering depth","Low to medium","Medium to high","High in the tested technical area"],["UX fidelity","Low to high","Enough to create a credible product experience","Usually low"],["Production readiness","No","Controlled and limited, depending on context","No"],["Best outcome","A clearer experience","Evidence about product value","Evidence about technical feasibility"]]},{"type":"callout","tone":"info","title":"The key distinction","text":"Prototype, MVP, and PoC are not ranked from 'small' to 'big'. They are ranked by the uncertainty they are designed to reduce."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Do not confuse feasibility, value, and readiness"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A common reason product discussions become confusing is that different teams are trying to answer different questions at the same time."},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["Desirability: do people understand and want the experience? A Prototype can provide evidence here.","Product value: does the product create useful behavior or outcomes? An MVP can provide evidence here.","Technical feasibility: can the intended approach work under the relevant constraints? A PoC can provide evidence here.","Operational readiness: can the solution be secured, monitored, supported, governed, and run reliably at the intended scale? This requires a broader delivery and production-readiness process."]},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A product can be desirable but technically infeasible. It can be technically feasible but not valuable. It can be valuable in a small test and still not be ready for enterprise-scale operation. Good validation keeps these claims separate."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Prototype vs MVP: the most common misunderstanding"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A polished prototype can look more complete than an MVP. That visual similarity is exactly why teams confuse them."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The distinction is not visual completeness. It is evidence. A prototype can be high-fidelity and still be a simulation. An MVP is a working product used to generate learning from real behavior."},{"type":"callout","tone":"neutral","title":"A simple test","text":"If your primary question can be answered without building the real product behavior, you may still be at Prototype level. If the question depends on real usage, transactions, workflows, or measurable behavior, you are closer to MVP territory."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"MVP vs PoC: product value is not technical feasibility"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A team can build a working API integration and still have no evidence that users care. Conversely, users can strongly want a solution that depends on an integration the current systems cannot support."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"That is why technical uncertainty and product uncertainty should not be collapsed into one experiment when doing so makes the evidence ambiguous. A small PoC can answer the technical question. A separate MVP can answer the product-value question."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"PoC vs Pilot: another distinction worth knowing"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A PoC asks whether an approach is feasible. A pilot asks whether a more complete solution works in a limited real-world setting with controlled users, processes, and operational conditions."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The Australian Government's current PoC guidance distinguishes PoCs, pilots, and production by purpose, users, metrics, infrastructure, and operational readiness. A PoC is an early experiment; a pilot is a limited real-world implementation; production is the operational service. [See the framework](https://www.digital.gov.au/policy/ai/AI-POC-to-scale/ai-transition-stages-and-dimensions)."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This distinction matters when a team says, 'The PoC worked, so we are ready to launch.' Technical feasibility is an important milestone, but it is not the same as operational readiness."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Decision framework: choose by uncertainty"},{"type":"image","url":"/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/decision-tree.svg","alt":"Decision tree for choosing Prototype, MVP, or Proof of Concept based on the biggest uncertainty","caption":"Start with the risk that could change the project decision. Then select the smallest credible experiment."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"1. Write the uncertainty as a question"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Replace vague statements such as 'the product is complex' with a testable question. For example: Can target users complete the approval flow without assistance? Will operations teams use the dashboard repeatedly? Can the legacy API deliver the required data within an acceptable response time?"},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"2. Identify the cost of being wrong"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Not every unknown deserves the same amount of validation. Prioritize the uncertainty that could cause the largest waste, rework, missed business opportunity, or failed delivery if you discover it too late."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"3. Choose the smallest credible experiment"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Use a Prototype, MVP, PoC, or a small combination. The objective is not to maximize features. It is to maximize decision value per unit of effort."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"4. Define the evidence before you start"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Write down what would strengthen the hypothesis, weaken it, or leave it unresolved. This prevents teams from changing the definition of success after seeing the result."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"5. Attach a decision to the result"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["Continue: the evidence is strong enough to justify the next investment.","Change direction: the hypothesis is weakened or a better path is visible.","Run another test: the evidence is useful but not decisive.","Stop: the risk is too high or the expected value is not strong enough."]},{"type":"image","url":"/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/framework.svg","alt":"Kapitech validation framework connecting product risk, user risk, technical risk, validation method, evidence, and next decision","caption":"A practical chain: risk → validation method → evidence → decision."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"How to write a strong validation hypothesis"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A useful hypothesis is specific enough to be challenged. A simple structure is:"},{"type":"callout","tone":"neutral","title":"Hypothesis template","text":"We believe [target user] will [behavior or outcome] because [reason]. We will treat the hypothesis as supported or weakened based on [evidence] under [defined context]."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Examples:"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["<strong>Prototype:</strong> We believe operations users can complete the approval task without assistance because the proposed information hierarchy matches the way they make decisions. We will test task success, confusion points, and time on task.","<strong>MVP:</strong> We believe operations teams will use the approval dashboard repeatedly because it removes manual status tracking. We will observe core-task completion, repeat usage, and time saved.","<strong>PoC:</strong> We believe the legacy API can support the real-time dashboard because the required data can be synchronized within the acceptable response window. We will test representative requests, latency, error rates, and data consistency."]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Example: a B2B Operations Platform"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Imagine a company wants to build a platform for approvals, workflow management, operational dashboards, reporting, and notifications. The feature list is large, but the real decision is which risks need evidence first."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Question A: Will people understand the workflow?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Start with a Prototype. Model the approval flow, status states, exceptions, and key decisions. Test representative users completing the most important tasks. Evidence should tell the team what to change in the experience before production engineering."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Question B: Will the product create enough value to change behavior?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Move to an MVP. Build only the core workflow needed to observe actual usage. Track whether users activate, complete the key job, return to the product, and experience a measurable improvement in the operational outcome that matters."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Question C: Can the legacy API support the experience?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Run a PoC. Do not build the full dashboard before proving the data path. Test representative requests, latency, failure behavior, authentication, and data consistency. If the integration fails the required constraints, the product architecture or scope can change before the larger build."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"This is often more efficient than treating Prototype → MVP → PoC as a mandatory checklist. The order should follow the risks, not a ceremony."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Common mistakes in product validation"},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"1. Building too much before asking the key question"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Teams add settings, dashboards, permissions, analytics, and edge cases before validating the core assumption. Scope grows while the most important uncertainty remains untouched."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"2. Calling a high-fidelity prototype an MVP"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A realistic interface can create a strong demo without producing evidence from real product usage. Visual fidelity is not the definition of viability."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"3. Using an MVP to answer a technical question"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"If an integration can invalidate the product, prove that dependency early. A narrow PoC can be cheaper and more informative than discovering the constraint after the main product is already built."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"4. Running a PoC without success criteria"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A PoC that ends with 'the demo worked' is difficult to evaluate. Define measurable technical conditions before the experiment begins."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"5. Measuring after the experiment instead of before it"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"When the success definition changes after the result, teams can rationalize almost any outcome. Write the evidence standard first."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"6. Trying to validate every uncertainty with one artifact"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A single deliverable can become overloaded with goals. Separate experiments often produce cleaner evidence and clearer decisions."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"7. Optimizing for deliverables instead of learning"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The number of screens, endpoints, or features is not the same as the amount of uncertainty removed. Optimize for learning velocity and decision quality."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"How we approach product validation at Kapitech"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"At Kapitech, we treat validation as a decision system. We first map the uncertainty across business, user experience, and technical delivery. Then we choose the smallest credible experiment that can reduce the highest-risk unknown."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"That may mean product framing and UX prototyping. It may mean a tightly scoped MVP. It may mean a technical PoC for an API, data source, AI capability, or architecture. It may also mean running two focused experiments in parallel."},{"type":"callout","tone":"success","title":"Kapitech principle","text":"Do not build more to feel certain. Build only enough to learn what you need to decide next."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The outcome we care about is not simply a polished artifact. It is a clearer decision: continue with confidence, change the approach, run another test, or stop before more budget is committed."},{"type":"link","label":"Discuss Your Product With Kapitech","url":"https://kapitech.id/contact"},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Practical checklist before you build"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["Who is the primary user, and what job are they trying to complete?","What is the single biggest uncertainty right now?","Is the uncertainty mainly about user experience, product value, technical feasibility, or operational readiness?","What evidence would change the roadmap decision?","What is the smallest experiment that can produce that evidence credibly?","What should be explicitly out of scope?","What would count as success, failure, or an inconclusive result?","What decision will be made when the experiment ends?"]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Frequently asked questions"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"These questions cover the decisions teams most often face when comparing Prototype, MVP, and Proof of Concept."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Is a prototype cheaper than an MVP?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Often, a prototype requires less engineering, but cost is not the right decision criterion by itself. A prototype is cheaper when it can answer the uncertainty you have without building more. A PoC may be the most cost-effective option when one technical dependency could invalidate a much larger product."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Can an MVP be built without a prototype?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Yes. If the user experience is already well understood and the main uncertainty is product value, a team may go directly to a tightly scoped MVP. A prototype is valuable when UX uncertainty is still material."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Can a PoC become the MVP?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Sometimes PoC code, findings, or integration work can inform the MVP, but the success criteria are different. A PoC proves feasibility in a defined technical area. An MVP is designed to generate product learning from real usage."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"How do I choose between a Prototype and a PoC?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Ask where the biggest unknown lives. If it is in the interaction, information, or workflow, prototype it. If it is in the system, data, integration, architecture, or performance, run a PoC."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Do I always need all three?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"No. Use only the validation methods required by the current risks. Use multiple methods when multiple uncertainties are both critical and independent."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"What should happen after validation?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Use the evidence to continue, change the scope, change the approach, run another test, or stop. Validation creates value when it changes a decision."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"How long should an MVP or PoC take?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"There is no universal duration. Scope, platform, integrations, data, design depth, technical constraints, and the evidence required all affect the work. A better rule is to timebox the experiment around the learning objective and avoid adding scope that does not improve the evidence."},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Does an MVP have to be production-ready?"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"An MVP needs to be viable for its intended experiment and user context, but it does not automatically need the full feature completeness, hardening, governance, and operational maturity of a mature enterprise product. The required level of readiness depends on the risk and environment."},{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Conclusion: choose the test, not the label"},{"type":"paragraph","text":"Prototype, MVP, and Proof of Concept are not competing versions of the same product. They are different ways of producing evidence."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"A Prototype helps you learn whether people can understand and use an experience. An MVP helps you learn whether a working product creates meaningful behavior and value. A PoC helps you learn whether a technical approach is feasible under relevant constraints."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"The strongest teams do not start by asking, 'Should we build an MVP or a Prototype?' They start with the uncertainty that could make the project fail, define the evidence they need, and choose the smallest credible experiment that can produce it."},{"type":"paragraph","text":"<strong>Choose the smallest thing that can answer your biggest uncertainty.</strong> That is the real purpose of product validation."},{"type":"link","label":"Explore Kapitech MVP & Product Design","url":"https://kapitech.id/solutions/mvp-design"},{"type":"link","label":"Start a Project With Kapitech","url":"https://kapitech.id/contact"},{"type":"divider"}]$$::jsonb,
  tags = $$[{"slug":"mvp","name":"MVP"},{"slug":"prototype","name":"Prototype"},{"slug":"proof-of-concept","name":"Proof of Concept"},{"slug":"product-validation","name":"Product Validation"},{"slug":"digital-product","name":"Digital Product"},{"slug":"product-discovery","name":"Product Discovery"},{"slug":"ux-design","name":"UX Design"}]$$::jsonb,
  status = 'published',
  is_featured = TRUE,
  reading_time = 14,
  key_takeaways = $$["A Prototype is best when the main uncertainty is the user experience: can people understand, navigate, and complete the core task?","An MVP is best when you need evidence from real product usage to test whether the core value creates meaningful user or business behavior.","A Proof of Concept (PoC) is best when a technical question could invalidate the wider product plan: integration, performance, data, architecture, AI, or infrastructure.","Prototype, MVP, and PoC are not mandatory stages. The right sequence depends on the risk in front of the team, and more than one method may be needed.","The strongest validation starts with a falsifiable question, a clear evidence threshold, and a decision attached to the result.","Do not confuse technical feasibility, product value, and production readiness. Passing one does not automatically prove the others.","The goal of validation is not to produce the largest artifact. It is to reduce the most expensive uncertainty with the least unnecessary work."]$$::jsonb,
  faq = $$[{"question":"What is the main difference between an MVP, Prototype, and PoC?","answer":"A Prototype primarily validates the user experience, an MVP generates evidence about product value through real usage, and a PoC validates a technical approach under relevant constraints."},{"question":"Is an MVP just a smaller version of the final product?","answer":"No. An MVP is scoped around a specific product hypothesis and must be viable enough to create a trustworthy learning signal. Features are removed because they are unnecessary for the current learning objective, not simply because they are expensive."},{"question":"Can a Prototype be high-fidelity and still not be an MVP?","answer":"Yes. Fidelity describes how closely a prototype resembles the intended experience. An MVP is defined by its purpose: learning from real product usage and measurable behavior."},{"question":"When is a Proof of Concept worth doing?","answer":"A PoC is worth doing when a technical unknown could materially change or invalidate the wider project and can be tested through a focused experiment."},{"question":"Can I use a Prototype and PoC at the same time?","answer":"Yes. If user-experience and technical feasibility are both critical, separate experiments can reduce those risks in parallel without forcing one artifact to answer both questions."},{"question":"Does a successful PoC mean the product is ready to launch?","answer":"No. A successful PoC shows that the tested technical approach is feasible. Launch readiness also requires product, security, operational, governance, support, and scale considerations."},{"question":"What metrics should I use for an MVP?","answer":"Use metrics tied to the product hypothesis, such as activation, core-task completion, repeat usage, conversion, willingness to pay, time saved, error reduction, or other meaningful outcomes. There is no universal MVP threshold."},{"question":"How should a business choose between Prototype, MVP, and PoC?","answer":"Start with the highest-risk uncertainty, define the evidence that would change the decision, then choose the smallest credible experiment that can produce that evidence."}]$$::jsonb,
  seo_title = "MVP vs Prototype vs Proof of Concept: Which Should Your Business Choose? | Kapitech",
  seo_description = "MVP vs Prototype vs PoC explained: what each validates, when to use it, what to measure, and how to choose the smallest test for your biggest product risk.",
  og_image = "https://kapitech.id/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept/hero.svg",
  canonical_url = "https://kapitech.id/insights/mvp-vs-prototype-vs-proof-of-concept-guide",
  updated_at = NOW()
WHERE slug = undefined
  AND language = 'en';

